  body: `This is the catch-up for the first three weeks of May — the period when the whole picture took shape. If you're just finding this page, start here.

## THE DEFINING DAY: MAY 12
On May 12 the province released the final design rules for ADAP and began sending letters to all roughly 80,000 AISH recipients telling each person whether they stay on AISH or move to ADAP on July 1. This is the announcement everything since has been about.

## WHAT THE RULES SET
ADAP's core benefit is $1,740 a month, $200 below AISH's $1,940. Anyone moved from AISH keeps their current amount through a $200 transition top-up that runs until December 31, 2027 — after that, the cut is permanent. New applicants after July 1 get only $1,740. From the August payment period, couples where both partners receive disability benefits each get 88 percent of the maximum, and child-benefit rates are recalibrated (the first child goes up, larger families come out behind). ADAP clients judged able to work must engage with employment supports. The province committed to cover the cost of one medical reassessment for anyone who later seeks to return to AISH.

## WHO STAYS, WHO MOVES
The province expects about 50,000 people to move to ADAP and about 30,000 to stay on AISH — those with severe or profound developmental disabilities, PDD recipients, palliative or terminal conditions, continuing-care residents, and people 60 and older.

## A NEW MINISTER, RIGHT BEFORE LAUNCH
On May 21 a cabinet shuffle handed the AISH/ADAP file to a new minister, Nathan Neudorf, just six weeks before launch. Jason Nixon, who had run the file and defended it, moved to Finance and Treasury Board.

That is where things stood at the end of May 21. From here, the daily updates pick it up day by day. We keep watching, and we keep going, together.`
